<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kupang</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kupang is the capital of the Indonesian province of East Nusa Tenggara, at the south-western end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timor">Timor</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> took the fort there in 1653, and it became the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> power on Timor in rivalry with the Portuguese-allied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/topasses">Topasses</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lifau">Lifau</a>. It remained the seat of Dutch <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-timor">West Timor</a> until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesian-independence">Indonesian independence</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kupang.jpg" alt="Kupang" width="320" height="174" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Government of East Nusa Tenggara · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sasando_Building_Kupang.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kupang lies on the south-western tip of the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timor">Timor</a>, with the Savu Sea on its northern shore. It is the largest city and port on the island, and the southernmost large city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>, as well as the closest to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>. Its position let the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> watch shipping along Timor's south coast, and the Koinino River supplied fresh water. The climate is tropical savanna, with extreme wet and dry seasons: January is the wettest month and August and September the driest.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>When representatives of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> (VOC) first encountered Kupang in 1613, after conquering the Portuguese fort on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solor">Solor</a>, the area was governed by a Raja of the Helong people. Because the VOC had little presence on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timor">Timor</a>, the Portuguese mestizo <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/topasses">Topasses</a> of Flores influenced the area strongly, and by the 1640s a Portuguese stronghold stood there. By 1646 the VOC was established on Solor and renewed its agreement with the Raja, and in January 1653 it built Fort Concordia on the left bank of the river estuary. Kupang then became the base of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> struggle against the Portuguese.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> defeats between 1655 and 1657, refugees from the allied Sonbai and Amabi principalities settled around the town on Helong land, followed by the Amfoang in 1683 and the Taebenu in 1688. The Helong Raja stayed Lord of the Land but depended closely on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">VOC</a>. In 1749 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/topasses">Topasses</a> were decisively defeated by the Dutch and their allies, which extended VOC influence over wide areas of western and central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timor">Timor</a>. Chinese traders and artisans had settled by the early 18th century and became indispensable to the local economy.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-bligh">William Bligh</a>, set adrift after the Mutiny on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hms-bounty">Bounty</a>, reached Kupang on 14 June 1789 after 41 days and 3,618 nautical miles in an open boat. The British attacked in 1797 and 1811 and were driven off, but Kupang surrendered in January 1812 after the British took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, and was returned to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> in 1816. It was opened to foreign trade in 1825 and was a free port after 1866. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> occupied it from 1942 to 1945, and Allied bombing destroyed much of the Old Town. It joined the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> in 1950.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Kupang is the capital of East Nusa Tenggara province and a city of 442,758 people at the 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. Its population is largely Christian, with Muslim, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> and Buddhist minorities. The University of Nusa Cendana is the oldest university in the province. It is the seat of an archdiocese, the diocese having been founded in 1967 and raised in 1989. Ruins and remnants of the colonial period survive in the city.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kupang” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By the 17th century it was drawn into the rivalry between the Portuguese-allied Topasses of Lifau and the Dutch East India Company at <b>Kupang</b>, and in the 1650s it split in two.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-sonbai">Kingdom of Sonbai</a></li></ul></section>
